WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.752 --> 00:00:04.504
Glad to have you guys on board, standby for comm check.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:08.048
The Falcon 9 showed the world what SpaceX was capable of.

00:00:08.204 --> 00:00:10.079
SpaceX, Dragon, loud and clear.

00:00:10.079 --> 00:00:13.890
It was the first time a private company sent astronauts into orbit.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.101
God speed Bob and Doug.

00:00:16.101 --> 00:00:22.453
Since that mission in May, four more astronauts boarded for the first operational crewed flight, Crew-1.

00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:28.069
The Falcon 9 may have put the spotlight on SpaceX, but it’s just the opening act before the main show.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.163
Starship.

00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:34.526
3, 2, 1, 0

00:00:35.604 --> 00:00:38.514
we have liftoff

00:00:38.514 --> 00:00:43.734
SpaceX hopes to journey far beyond Earth on what will be the world's most powerful launch vehicle.

00:00:46.979 --> 00:00:53.079
In December, a Starship prototype, Serial Number 8, took off from its launch pad in Boca Chica, Texas

00:00:53.079 --> 00:00:59.168
It flew farther than any of its predecessors - soaring to 12.5 kilometers or 41,000 feet.

00:00:59.168 --> 00:01:00.966
So far, so good.

00:01:00.966 --> 00:01:10.424
But returning was tricky. Starship performed a belly flop maneuver. The unusual execution allows Starship to better control the landing by using its fins.

00:01:10.424 --> 00:01:14.982
It didn’t make much of a difference in this case because the vehicle crash-landed.

00:01:20.409 --> 00:01:23.813
Musk had given the flight a one in three chance of success.

00:01:23.813 --> 00:01:28.970
Despite the epic ending, it was still considered successful. Incredible work, team. Nice work. 

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.330
Because Musk said SpaceX had collected all the data it needed.

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:40.245
This is the farthest by far that Starship has gone. Previous prototypes only performed tiny hops of 150 meters.

00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:44.408
The part that flew was only the spacecraft, the second stage, which will

00:01:44.408 --> 00:01:49.596
eventually sit on top of the first stage for a trip to space - a rocket known as the Super Heavy.

00:01:49.596 --> 00:01:55.182
It’s collectively called Starship, and towers 120 meters or 394 feet.

00:01:55.182 --> 00:01:59.943
After the two stages separate, the Super Heavy returns to Earth by landing vertically

00:01:59.943 --> 00:02:04.163
while Starship journeys onward to its destination, before also coming home.

00:02:04.163 --> 00:02:07.730
What makes Starship unique is that it’s totally reusable.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:12.634
The Falcon 9 on the other hand is only partially reusable. The booster returns.

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:18.651
SpaceX envisions Starship being similar to a plane, with constant flights and quick turnaround times.

00:02:18.651 --> 00:02:24.740
It will have to fly hundreds of times without a crew, at least until takeoffs and landings are perfected.

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:31.555
Then, it might be able to take a bite out of the commercial airline industry by transporting travelers from place to place on Earth.

00:02:31.555 --> 00:02:35.834
The company envisions going from Sydney to Singapore in 31 minutes.

00:02:35.834 --> 00:02:38.562
LA to Toronto in just 24.

00:02:38.562 --> 00:02:41.599
Shorter distances may not even require the booster.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.585
The spacecraft could travel on its own, powered by 6 Raptor engines. 

00:02:45.585 --> 00:02:48.524
The prototype that flew had three of those engines on board.

00:02:48.524 --> 00:02:53.620
Earth to Earth travel may be an easier milestone. But the real focus is farther away. 

00:02:55.079 --> 00:02:58.329
No one has been to the moon since 1972.

00:02:58.329 --> 00:03:05.689
When NASA sends the next man and first woman back by 2024, it will have to decide which company will get them there.

00:03:05.689 --> 00:03:09.694
SpaceX has competition from both Blue Origin and Dynetics.

00:03:09.694 --> 00:03:16.257
It wants to modify Starship for the historic trip - it certainly doesn’t look like any type of lunar lander we’ve seen before.

00:03:16.257 --> 00:03:21.720
Sending people to the moon is a tremendous achievement in and of itself but for SpaceX,

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:27.835
this is simply a step, an opportunity to gain experience before achieving an even greater dream.

00:03:27.835 --> 00:03:31.427
It’s no secret SpaceX is trying to get to Mars.

00:03:31.427 --> 00:03:36.302
I’m optimistic about the future on Earth but it’s important to have life insurance for life as a whole.

00:03:36.302 --> 00:03:43.795
Beyond the insurance policy, there is an even bigger reason, which he talked about during a recent event in Berlin.

00:03:43.795 --> 00:03:46.476
We want to have a future that is inspiring and exciting.

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:50.173
A future where we are a space-faring civilization and out there among the stars.

00:03:50.173 --> 00:03:54.589
I think that's, every kid gets excited about that. You don’t even need to teach them.

00:03:54.589 --> 00:03:59.036
Dreams of humans settling on the red planet will depend on Starship’s rate of innovation.

00:03:59.036 --> 00:04:03.701
Musk hopes an uncrewed mission to Mars could occur as early as 2022.

00:04:03.701 --> 00:04:09.434
And he’s highly confident humans could get there in 2026 or possibly 2024.

00:04:09.434 --> 00:04:13.216
100 passengers will brace themselves for a six-month journey.

00:04:13.216 --> 00:04:17.421
As difficult as it is to get to Mars, the real work begins upon arrival. 

00:04:17.421 --> 00:04:23.661
Because a city needs constructing, especially if the vision is a million people living there by 2050.

00:04:23.661 --> 00:04:27.853
A fleet of a thousand Starships will be charged with the daunting task.

00:04:27.853 --> 00:04:33.893
This will be an incredibly expensive undertaking, at least $100 billion but possibly trillions. 

00:04:33.893 --> 00:04:38.957
SpaceX is going to need boatloads of cash. Or in this case, shiploads.

00:04:38.957 --> 00:04:47.052
Its new satellite internet service for Earth called Starlink is projected to bring in $30 billion dollars a year of desperately needed money.

00:04:47.052 --> 00:04:51.086
What an awesome view with the sunlight hitting those Starlink satellites.

00:04:51.086 --> 00:04:56.767
The Falcon 9 has been launching the satellites but it can only carry 60 Starlink satellites at a time.

00:04:56.767 --> 00:05:02.547
Since SpaceX wants to build a constellation of at least 12,000 that means about 200 flights.

00:05:02.547 --> 00:05:09.832
And if the company wants to extend that to a full 42,000 one day, 700 launches is no small feat.

00:05:09.832 --> 00:05:13.862
That’s why SpaceX plans on using Starship to carry Starlink in the future. 

00:05:13.862 --> 00:05:17.159
Instead of 60 satellites, it can take 400.

00:05:17.159 --> 00:05:22.415
Its payload capacity is over 100 metric tons or about the weight of a blue whale.

00:05:22.741 --> 00:05:29.555
Eventually, satellites would also orbit Mars to provide internet to its new inhabitants. Musk hopes to be one of them.

00:05:29.725 --> 00:05:31.695
And you seriously want to be buried on Mars?

00:05:32.517 --> 00:05:34.055
 Just not on impact. Haha.

00:05:36.606 --> 00:05:44.564
Listen, we’re all gonna die someday. So, if you're gonna die someday, like, okay, do you want to be buried on Mars or Earth? I’m like, Mars sounds cool.

00:05:44.564 --> 00:05:48.047
Born on Earth, die on Mars. That’s, y’know, if you’ve got the choice.

00:05:49.322 --> 00:05:55.424
Every step along the way of his plan to live and be buried on Mars depends on the success of Starship. 

00:05:55.424 --> 00:06:00.746
With rapid improvements, this beast of a vehicle will one day journey far beyond Earth.

00:06:00.746 --> 00:06:04.256
Or in the words of Musk: “Mars, here we come!!”

00:06:04.256 --> 00:06:11.648
